The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
void devlink_free(struct devlink *devlink)
{
	ASSERT_DEVLINK_NOT_REGISTERED(devlink);
	// ... (missing devlink_rel_put(devlink)) ...
}
```
Reason:  
The nested relation (`devlink->rel`) was only released inside `devl_unregister()` via `devlink_rel_put()`. When a devlink instance (e.g., an SF) acquires a nested relation before registration but then fails probe, `devlink_free()` is called without prior `devl_unregister()`. This skips the relation release, causing a leak of `devlink->rel`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
void devlink_free(struct devlink *devlink)
{
	ASSERT_DEVLINK_NOT_REGISTERED(devlink);
	devlink_rel_put(devlink);   // added line
	// ... rest of function ...
}
```
Reason:  
By adding `devlink_rel_put(devlink)` at the start of `devlink_free()`, the nested relation is released regardless of whether `devl_unregister()` was called. For the registered path, `devl_unregister()` already clears and puts the relation before `devlink_free()` runs, so the extra call is a no-op (safe double put is handled internally). This ensures the relation is properly freed even when probe fails before registration.

